WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.503
I've sort of coined a phrase, which is,

00:00:02.628 --> 00:00:04.671
"Get the audience at the edge
of their seat,

00:00:04.796 --> 00:00:06.924
and while they're leaning forward hit 'em with the truth."

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:10.260
I didn't really know

00:00:10.385 --> 00:00:13.805
what it was, I think,
until I was about 18 years old.

00:00:13.931 --> 00:00:17.434
You know, I would hear it,
but I didn't really fully grasp

00:00:17.559 --> 00:00:19.478
the magnitude of it.

00:00:19.603 --> 00:00:20.938
What is Juneteenth?

00:00:21.063 --> 00:00:25.234
In Galveston, Texas in 1865,
about two and a half years

00:00:25.359 --> 00:00:29.112
after Lincoln signed
The Emancipation Proclamation in 1863,

00:00:29.238 --> 00:00:33.200
was the time at which Black people
understood that they were also free.

00:00:33.325 --> 00:00:34.785
Enslaved Black people.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:37.913
And so, some stories go
that they had been held there

00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:43.043
in order to keep farming the land
until the harvest season was over.

00:00:43.168 --> 00:00:46.797
Juneteenth is a really special holiday
for African Americans.

00:00:46.922 --> 00:00:50.133
I grew up in the South
and was aware of it at a young age.

00:00:50.259 --> 00:00:55.430
It is the celebration of the point
at which all Black people were free.

00:00:55.556 --> 00:00:59.226
People refer to it
as the second Independence Day.

00:00:59.351 --> 00:01:03.730
I see it as the first Independence Day;
none of us are free until we all are.

00:01:03.856 --> 00:01:06.608
So how did you come to do
&lt;i&gt;Lincoln's Dilemma?&lt;/i&gt;

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:08.861
It was the opportunity to impact,

00:01:08.986 --> 00:01:11.321
to add to the kind of canon of stories

00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:14.825
about a really important
and really key president.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.620
What you get across this county
is a history

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:22.916
that is often not robust and complex
around the stories of Black people.

00:01:23.041 --> 00:01:26.461
Black people really pushed the country
towards emancipation.

00:01:26.587 --> 00:01:30.507
There were so many actions done,
even by enslaved people,

00:01:30.632 --> 00:01:34.094
exerting as much power as they had
in their day-to-day lives

00:01:34.219 --> 00:01:36.221
to really push all of the country

00:01:36.346 --> 00:01:39.141
to understand
and embrace their humanity.

00:01:39.266 --> 00:01:43.604
I'm just curious about how this history
informs the work that you do

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:45.564
and, in particular, talk about &lt;i&gt;Swagger.&lt;/i&gt;

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:49.526
The opening image of &lt;i&gt;Swagger&lt;/i&gt;
is a maze. Right?

00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:52.196
And so, I think
for many audience members,

00:01:52.321 --> 00:01:54.364
that might just be a good visual.

00:01:54.489 --> 00:01:57.075
But in our show,
maze becomes a metaphor for life.

00:01:57.201 --> 00:02:02.748
But really, the maze ties back
into, like, the Harlem Renaissance,

00:02:02.873 --> 00:02:06.084
Reconstruction, emancipation,

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:09.254
and wanting to feel that
in the narrative.

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:11.089
I've sort of coined a phrase,

00:02:11.215 --> 00:02:14.009
which is, "Get the audience
at the edge of their seat,

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:16.178
then hit them with the truth."

00:02:16.303 --> 00:02:18.639
It's important for me
to tell the stories

00:02:18.764 --> 00:02:21.266
of Black folks in particular.

00:02:21.391 --> 00:02:24.144
Women, folks from the South,
that's all my demographic.

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:26.271
It's because those stories
are not uplifted,

00:02:26.396 --> 00:02:28.273
young Black girls, for example.

00:02:28.398 --> 00:02:31.527
It's a charge that I gladly take on,
and, at the same time,

00:02:31.652 --> 00:02:35.906
it just gives us so much more work to do
than the average director, right?

00:02:36.031 --> 00:02:39.743
It's interesting to hear you say
that you think of a social cause

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:42.621
when you're taking on a project.
That's all I do.

00:02:42.746 --> 00:02:44.873
-Yes.
-It doesn't matter what...

00:02:44.998 --> 00:02:48.210
It doesn't matter if it's about
basketball, that's all I do.

00:02:48.335 --> 00:02:53.799
The first thing that really
dropped in for me with &lt;i&gt;Swagger&lt;/i&gt;

00:02:53.924 --> 00:02:58.387
is that it was about 14-year-old
youth basketball players.

00:02:58.512 --> 00:03:00.389
What I took hold on right now

00:03:00.514 --> 00:03:03.892
is the most famous 14-year-old
in American history: Emmett Till.

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:07.521
There's an amazing picture of
Emmett Till by artist Lisa Whittington.

00:03:07.646 --> 00:03:12.401
Half of the painting is Emmett looking
like the world is his, optimistic.

00:03:12.526 --> 00:03:16.029
And the other side
is, like, marred and scarred

00:03:16.154 --> 00:03:19.825
and really taking on
all of the atrocities of the world.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.201
This is the side,

00:03:21.326 --> 00:03:26.248
the positive, the optimistic,
the side that we all fight for,

00:03:26.373 --> 00:03:30.127
for our kids, for our communities,
this is what &lt;i&gt;Swagger &lt;/i&gt;is about.

00:03:30.252 --> 00:03:31.545
I'm glad you mentioned that.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.339
I think about when I was looking
at &lt;i&gt;Lincoln's Dilemma&lt;/i&gt;

00:03:34.464 --> 00:03:35.924
and looking at the story,

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:39.595
and really writing scenes
about enslaved people

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:44.725
in a way that I wanted to make sure
that they weren't these flat narratives.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:48.228
I wanted to make sure they were infused
with all the humanity they had.

00:03:48.353 --> 00:03:51.315
It's very interesting
that that's my charge

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:54.902
in 2021 when I was doing the writing.

00:03:55.027 --> 00:03:58.906
I still need to communicate,
to other people,

00:03:59.031 --> 00:04:00.574
the humanity of Black folks.

00:04:00.699 --> 00:04:04.578
And so, it is still necessary,
it's a necessary charge.

00:04:04.703 --> 00:04:09.708
Did the events of 2020 impact
any of your recent work?

00:04:09.833 --> 00:04:13.045
It took me a moment,
but I got recommitted to my craft

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.255
and recommitted
to telling these stories.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:20.052
For me, there's a gift to Black people
when I'm telling their stories

00:04:20.177 --> 00:04:22.513
that I think I'm obligated to give.

00:04:22.638 --> 00:04:25.516
But the work that was driving me:

00:04:25.641 --> 00:04:29.144
I imagined that there might be
some person watching,

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:32.648
definitely some Black person
who sees themselves differently,

00:04:32.773 --> 00:04:36.151
sees their history differently
by understanding, you know,

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:40.531
simple things like when enslaved people
were working in the fields,

00:04:40.656 --> 00:04:42.574
they would sing songs.

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:44.201
You think about the songs

00:04:44.326 --> 00:04:47.371
and you often think about songs
and entertainment

00:04:47.496 --> 00:04:49.831
as a little bit of this, kind of, ways

00:04:49.957 --> 00:04:51.917
{\an2}that people like
to characterize Black folks

00:04:52.042 --> 00:04:55.712
as, like, shuffling and singing
stereotype, right?

00:04:55.838 --> 00:04:59.007
But enslaved people sang songs
in the field, work songs,

00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:01.510
to change the tempo of their work.

00:05:01.635 --> 00:05:04.304
They would sing slower
to slow things down.

00:05:04.429 --> 00:05:07.724
If somebody on the end of the line,
at the end of that row,

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:10.269
needed to catch up or wasn't well,

00:05:10.394 --> 00:05:12.437
they would slow them down
or speed it up.

00:05:12.563 --> 00:05:14.898
So those were actual choices

00:05:15.023 --> 00:05:17.526
in the ways Black people
organized with each other.

00:05:17.651 --> 00:05:21.113
Like you, after the events in 2020,

00:05:21.238 --> 00:05:22.865
production shut down,

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:25.075
just because of the pandemic.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.744
I think during that shutdown,

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:30.789
initially my thought was
that when we'd come back,

00:05:30.914 --> 00:05:36.295
we'd continue the season on as is
and just kind of stay the course.

00:05:37.045 --> 00:05:38.714
Just hearing various opinions

00:05:38.839 --> 00:05:43.177
that people didn't really want to deal
with the pandemic on-screen

00:05:43.302 --> 00:05:46.013
or the events of, you know, George Floyd

00:05:46.138 --> 00:05:48.307
-or Breonna or any of that on-screen.
-Yes.

00:05:48.432 --> 00:05:51.393
And then it just felt like
it's a missed opportunity.

00:05:51.518 --> 00:05:53.478
I'm an artist, I'm a Black artist,

00:05:53.604 --> 00:05:56.940
and I have to hold a mirror up
to society right now.

00:05:57.065 --> 00:06:01.069
I felt like one of the better decisions
I made on the show was that

00:06:01.195 --> 00:06:03.655
we embraced the fact
that we were in a pandemic

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:08.327
and we embraced the fact that we
were all impacted by these murders.

00:06:08.452 --> 00:06:10.829
It felt like it elevated
everything we were doing.

00:06:10.954 --> 00:06:14.625
I think that there is
a kind of leveling out,

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:17.586
as opposed to the exceptionalism
that's often used

00:06:17.711 --> 00:06:20.172
to refer to the way
that Black folks show up.

00:06:20.297 --> 00:06:24.551
A leveling out and an understanding of
what we do in the spaces that we're in.

00:06:24.676 --> 00:06:28.805
Like you said, we're filmmakers, so this
is the space in which we have power,

00:06:28.931 --> 00:06:31.475
and it's really important
to leverage that.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:34.561
And particularly
when there's backlash at times,

00:06:34.686 --> 00:06:36.313
which has certainly been the case.

00:06:36.438 --> 00:06:39.316
That's great, I think
one of the things that can get lost,

00:06:39.441 --> 00:06:42.819
even sometimes when,
this conversation's about Juneteenth,

00:06:42.945 --> 00:06:45.864
-is, like, how great it is to be Black.
-Right?

00:06:45.989 --> 00:06:49.576
Like, how much of our culture
is a celebration,

00:06:49.701 --> 00:06:52.204
how much of our culture
has influenced this country.

00:06:52.329 --> 00:06:54.289
-Yeah.
-How much of, like,

00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:56.416
Black love has sustained us.

00:06:56.542 --> 00:06:58.502
It's sustained us, and this country.

00:06:58.627 --> 00:07:01.505
We wouldn't be a democracy
without the work that folks did

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:06.134
to really push Lincoln along
to The Emancipation Proclamation.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:08.220
There's not another proclamation coming.

00:07:08.345 --> 00:07:11.014
-We have to write that proclamation now.
-Yes.

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:16.103
And it doesn't absolve other people
of their responsibilities.

00:07:16.228 --> 00:07:18.146
We just have to keep moving it forward.

00:07:18.272 --> 00:07:21.441
And we do have to stand
on the shoulders of ancestors,

00:07:21.567 --> 00:07:25.070
and we have to be the shoulders
that other people can stand on.

